    <body>
      <sec id="article-20808.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Dyspnea is a subjective respiratory sensation characterized by insufficient air intake arising from complex physiologic, psychological, social, and environmental interactions. Acute presentations occur in pulmonary embolism, asthma exacerbation, pneumonia, or heart failure, whereas chronic manifestations develop in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, interstitial lung disease, obesity, or neuromuscular weakness. The symptom may appear as air hunger, chest constriction, tachypnea, or increased respiratory effort, often restricting activity and diminishing functional capacity. Dyspnea is a clinical manifestation rather than a discrete disease entity.</p>
        <p>A structured,&#x000a0;interprofessional evaluation grounded in detailed history, comprehensive physical examination, and condition-specific diagnostic studies&#x02014;such as chest radiography, computed tomography, electrocardiography, complete blood count, arterial blood gas analysis, pulse oximetry, pulmonary function testing, or echocardiography&#x02014;supports accurate identification of underlying pathology. Recognition of immediately life-threatening etiologies, including tension pneumothorax, acute airway obstruction, acute myocardial infarction, or massive pulmonary embolism, is essential. Evidence-based interventions targeted to the precipitating disorder, optimization of comorbid conditions, and ongoing reassessment improve clinical outcomes and reduce morbidity.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in the evaluation and management of dyspnea by improving diagnostic accuracy, differentiating among cardiac, pulmonary, and nonorganic causes, and applying evidence-based treatment strategies. Learners will strengthen their ability to interpret clinical findings, implement appropriate diagnostic pathways, and coordinate interprofessional care. Participants will also gain a clearer understanding of the prognostic significance of dyspnea across disease states, approaches that support patient-centered management, and communication methods that aid individuals experiencing this clinically significant symptom.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Determine the etiologic factors contributing to acute and chronic dyspnea to optimize patient care.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply guideline-directed therapies to manage dyspnea across acute and chronic settings, ensuring appropriate pharmacologic and nonpharmacologic interventions.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient understanding of how early recognition of symptoms and compliance with therapy reduce complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate ongoing care and follow-up for patients with chronic dyspnea, integrating disease management, patient education, and supportive therapies across care settings.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=20808&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=20808">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-20808.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Dyspnea, commonly described as shortness of breath, is a subjective sensation of uncomfortable or difficult breathing arising from diverse medical, environmental, psychological, and physiological factors. Patients frequently report chest tightness, air hunger, rapid breathing, or a sensation of insufficient air. Dyspnea affects millions worldwide and may serve as the primary manifestation of respiratory, cardiac, neuromuscular, systemic, or psychogenic disorders, or a combination thereof. Psychosocial, cultural, and physiologic factors, including anxiety, depression, hormonal status, obesity, and overall fitness level, influence the perception and severity of dyspnea. Clinically, acute dyspnea develops over hours to days, subacute dyspnea evolves over several days up to 4 weeks, and chronic dyspnea persists for more than 4 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Effective evaluation requires a systematic approach, beginning with a detailed history and physical examination, supplemented by targeted diagnostics such as chest imaging, electrocardiography (ECG), and limited laboratory testing. Additional assessment may include pulmonary function tests, echocardiography, or advanced imaging guided by clinical suspicion. Management prioritizes identification and treatment of the underlying cause&#x02014;whether optimizing heart failure therapy, addressing airway obstruction, or providing psychological support&#x02014;while ensuring symptom relief and patient safety through coordinated,&#x000a0;interprofessional care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-20808.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Dyspnea, whether acute or chronic, is a symptom of an underlying condition rather than a distinct disease.&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;etiologies&#x000a0;may be broadly classified into&#x000a0;6 primary categories: respiratory, cardiac, neuromuscular, psychogenic, systemic, or multifactorial.</p>
        <p>Acute dyspnea is most commonly due to either pulmonary or cardiovascular causes. Respiratory causes can be further categorized as diseases of the upper airway, chest wall, diaphragm, or lung parenchyma. Such organization allows clinicians to prioritize potentially life-threatening conditions and streamline evaluation.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Upper airway diseases: Laryngeal mass, vocal cord paralysis, inducible laryngeal obstruction, goiter or neck mass compressing the airway</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chest wall diseases: Kyphoscoliosis, obesity, ventral hernia, ascites</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Diaphragmatic disorders: Diaphragmatic paralysis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary diseases: Asthma, bronchiectasis, bronchiolitis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), interstitial lung disease, pleural effusion, hemothorax, pulmonary hypertension, venous thromboembolism, pulmonary right-to-left shunt, airway occlusion due to a pulmonary mass, bronchospasm, pneumothorax, pneumonia, aspiration, anaphylaxis, bronchitis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Cardiovascular causes represent a significant category of dyspnea, as cardiac dysfunction can impair oxygen delivery and hemodynamic stability, often leading to acute or chronic shortness of breath. Prompt recognition of these conditions is critical, as delayed diagnosis or management can result in serious morbidity or mortality. Common cardiovascular etiologies include arrhythmia, cardiomyopathy, cardiac tamponade, congestive heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, coronary artery disease (CAD), deconditioning, intracardiac shunt, restrictive cardiomyopathy, and valvular dysfunction.</p>
        <p>Other pathologies that can result in breathing difficulty are classified as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Neuromuscular diseases: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, phrenic nerve dysfunction, glycolytic enzyme dysfunction, mitochondrial diseases, polymyositis or dermatomyositis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Toxic, metabolic, or systemic causes: Anemia, carbon monoxide poisoning, metabolic acidosis, renal failure, thyroid disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r6">[6]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Miscellaneous disorders: Anxiety, pregnancy, gastroesophageal reflux disease, trauma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The diverse causes of dyspnea necessitate a structured approach to diagnosis, with attention to the specific etiology guiding appropriate testing and interventions. Recognition of the underlying mechanism also informs prognosis and ongoing care planning.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-20808.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Dyspnea is a common reason for emergency department visits, representing roughly 5% of cases each year. The leading causes include lower respiratory tract infection, heart failure, COPD, and asthma, which together account for the majority of presentations.&#x000a0;The relative frequency of these conditions varies with patient age and comorbidities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Lower respiratory tract infections, particularly pneumonia, contribute to 20% to 26% of cases, making them the single most common cause in many studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r8">[8]</xref> Heart failure accounts for 15% to 28% of presentations, with the highest rates observed in adults older than 75.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r9">[9]</xref> COPD exacerbations represent 13% to 18%, typically affecting older adults and individuals with a history of smoking, whereas asthma exacerbations predominate among younger adults and comprise 13% to 15% of visits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-20808.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Some studies show that COVID-19 has been responsible for up to 17% of dyspnea presentations in recent years. Other, less frequent etiologies include pulmonary embolism, acute coronary syndrome (ACS), anemia, and psychogenic dyspnea, each contributing to fewer than 5% of cases.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-20808.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Neurophysiological Mechanisms of Dyspnea</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The severity and presentation of dyspnea can vary independently of measurable physiologic changes, arising from complex interactions among central and peripheral neural pathways.&#x000a0;The symptom&#x000a0;reflects the integration of afferent signals from chemoreceptors and mechanoreceptors with motor command signals within the central nervous system. Both the automatic (brainstem-mediated) and voluntary (cortical) components of the respiratory motor system contribute to the perception and modulation of breathing effort.</p>
        <p>Mechanoreceptors in the airways, lungs, and chest wall provide continuous sensory feedback to respiratory centers regarding lung volume and airway status. Three primary types of pulmonary mechanoreceptors are involved: slow-adapting stretch receptors, rapidly adapting or irritant receptors, and juxtacapillary (J) receptors.</p>
        <p>Slow-adapting stretch receptors convey information about lung inflation, helping to regulate tidal volume and inhibit overexpansion (Hering&#x02013;Breuer reflex). Rapidly adapting receptors respond to changes in lung volume and to chemical or mechanical irritants, such as inhaled particles or inflammatory mediators. C fibers, originating from J-receptors in the alveolar walls near pulmonary capillaries, are activated by both mechanical and chemical stimuli. These afferents travel via the vagus nerve to the brainstem, influencing respiratory rate, depth, and reflexes such as cough.</p>
        <p>Peripheral chemoreceptors, located in the carotid bodies at the bifurcation of the common carotid arteries and in the aortic bodies of the aortic arch, primarily monitor arterial oxygen tension. The sensitivity of these&#x000a0;receptors increases in hypercapnia and acidosis, augmenting ventilatory drive under hypoxic conditions. Activation triggers afferent impulses via the glossopharyngeal nerve to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla, stimulating excitatory respiratory neurons and increasing ventilation. Carotid bodies contribute approximately 15% of total ventilatory drive under normal conditions and assume a dominant role during hypoxemia.</p>
        <p>Central chemoreceptors exert primary control over baseline respiratory drive. Located on the ventral surface of the medulla and within the retrotrapezoid nucleus, these receptors respond to changes in cerebrospinal fluid pH, which reflects arterial carbon dioxide (CO2) levels. C02 diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier, forming carbonic acid and lowering cerebrospinal fluid pH, which stimulates ventilation. Increases in pH, reflecting hypocapnia, suppress ventilation. This tightly regulated feedback system maintains stable arterial CO2 and pH levels during changing metabolic demands. Table 1 summarizes the primary sensory afferent sources involved in respiratory sensation and their clinical relevance.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-20808.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1.&#x000a0;Sensory Afferent Pathways Involved in the Perception of Dyspnea</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Afferent Source</bold>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
